# Local Obsidian editor config (per-machine, not part of the project)
.obsidian/

# Node (defensive; frontend/ has its own .gitignore too)
node_modules/
dist/

# Java / Maven build output
target/

# Secrets. docker/.env holds real credentials; only the example is committed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The F0 seed runner. It is written to hold the Owner password in plain text between being
# filled in and being deleted, and it sits in the repository root where `git add -A` would
# find it. Ignored so that window cannot end in a commit. See docs/HISTORY.md's F0 section.
/seed.ps1

# Claude Code's permission rules. SPLIT BY U5, 2026-08-07 — W1's decision is NOT reversed, it is
# scoped to the half it was ever about.
#
# W1's argument, 2026-08-04, quoted so it is not lost: this file "is a statement about what one
# developer's agent may run on one machine, not about this project — committing it would make one
# person's local trust decisions everybody's, and silently."
#
# ⚠️ That argument reaches ALLOW rules and does not reach ASK rules, because an ask rule GRANTS
# NOTHING — it only restricts. Anthropic's documentation makes the same split structurally: a
# project settings.json's `allow` rules take effect only after the workspace-trust dialog is
# accepted, while deny and ask rules "aren't affected, since they only restrict"
# (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions, read 2026-08-07).
#
# So `.claude/settings.json` is now COMMITTED and holds the `ask` array only — see D-U5.9/D-U5.10
# in CLAUDE.md for why one of those rules has to travel with the repository rather than live on one
# laptop. `allow` moved to `.claude/settings.local.json`, which stays ignored for W1's reason.
.claude/settings.local.json

# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
